<section class="essay-section essay-continuation" id="representations"><div class="prose">
 <h2>Representations and invariant subspaces</h2>
 <p>I find it useful to think of representation theory as a generalization of diagonalization. An eigenvector identifies a direction that a transformation preserves. For a whole group of transformations, we ask which subspaces are preserved by all of them.</p>
 <p>A representation is a map <Math tex={"R:G\\to U(V)"}/> preserving composition:</p>
 <div class="formula-pair"><Math display tex={"R(g_1g_2)=R(g_1)R(g_2)"}/><Math display tex={"R(e)=I."}/></div>
 <p>Here we use finite-dimensional complex unitary representations. For finite groups, or continuous representations of compact groups, choosing an invariant inner product lets us work in this setting.</p>
 <p>A subspace <Math tex={String.raw`W\subseteq V`}/> is <em>invariant</em> if every matrix <Math tex="R(g)"/> maps the subspace into itself: <Math tex={String.raw`R(g)W\subseteq W`}/> for all <Math tex={String.raw`g\in G`}/>. An irreducible representation, or irrep, has no proper nonzero invariant subspace. We can decompose the whole space into such pieces, using one basis change that makes every representation matrix block diagonal. Noncommuting matrices need not reduce all the way to one-dimensional blocks.</p>
 <p>For an abelian group, the complex irreps are one-dimensional. Translations around a cycle are a familiar example: the Fourier modes are common eigenvectors, and translating multiplies each mode by a phase. In this sense, irrep types generalize frequencies.</p>
 <h3>Multiplicity and the commutant</h3>
 <p>Take <Math tex={String.raw`\mathbb Z_2=\{e,s\}`}/>, with <Math tex="s^2=e"/>, and let it act on <Math tex={String.raw`V=\mathbb C^3`}/>. A representation assigns a matrix to each of the two group elements. Here those <Math tex={String.raw`3\times3`}/> matrices are</p>
 <div class="formula-pair"><Math display tex={String.raw`R(e)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}`} /><Math display tex={String.raw`R(s)=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0&0\\0&-1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.`} /></div>
 <p>The identity acts as <Math tex="I_3"/>, and <Math tex="R(s)^2=R(e)"/>, as required by the group multiplication. The one-dimensional sign representation occurs twice: <Math tex="s"/> acts by −1 on each of the first two coordinate lines. The third coordinate carries the trivial representation, with both elements acting by +1.</p>
 <p>The two-dimensional minus-sign eigenspace is determined by the representation, but its decomposition into two invariant lines is not unique. Any orthonormal basis of that plane gives two equivalent copies of the sign representation.</p>
 <p>We separate the type of irrep from how many copies occur:</p>
 <div class="formula-pair"><Math display tex={"V\\cong\\bigoplus_\\lambda V_\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda"}/><Math display tex={"R(g)\\cong\\bigoplus_\\lambda R_\\lambda(g)\\otimes I_{M_\\lambda}."}/></div>
 <p>The direct sum <Math tex={"\\bigoplus"}/> collects distinct irrep types. The tensor product <Math tex={"\\otimes"}/> records copies of one type: <Math tex={"\\dim M_\\lambda"}/> is its multiplicity. Dimensions add across the sum and multiply within a term. The full block containing one type is called its <em>isotypic component</em>; it is canonical even when a basis of the multiplicity space is not.</p>
 <p>What can an operator do while commuting with every <Math tex="R(g)"/>? Schur’s lemma says it can act on the multiplicity space while leaving the irreducible factor alone:</p>
 <Math display tex={"T=\\bigoplus_\\lambda I_{V_\\lambda}\\otimes T_\\lambda."}/>
 <p>These operators form the <em>commutant</em> of the representation. They can mix equivalent copies of an irrep, but cannot mix inequivalent irrep types. <Cite keys="childs-quantum-algorithms-notes"/></p>
</div></section>
<section class="essay-section essay-continuation" id="schur-weyl"><div class="prose">
 <h2>Schur–Weyl duality</h2>
 <p>On <Math tex="n"/> qubits, rotate every qubit by the same <Math tex={"U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)"}/>, or permute the qubits by <Math tex={"\\pi\\in S_n"}/>. The order of these two operations does not matter:</p>
 <Math display tex={"P(\\pi)U^{\\otimes n}=U^{\\otimes n}P(\\pi)."}/>
 <p>Reordering identical factors before rotating is the same as rotating before reordering. This does not make all the operations commute with one another: two permutations may fail to commute, as may two rotations.</p>
 <p>Schur–Weyl duality says more than this commutation relation. The algebras generated by the two actions are mutual commutants. Their common decomposition is</p>
 <Math display tex={"(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes n}\\cong\\bigoplus_J V_J\\otimes M_J."}/>
 <div class="formula-pair"><Math display tex={"U^{\\otimes n}\\cong\\bigoplus_J D_J(U)\\otimes I_{M_J}"}/><Math display tex={"P(\\pi)\\cong\\bigoplus_J I_{V_J}\\otimes D_J^{S_n}(\\pi)."}/></div>
 <p>Collective rotations act on the spin-<Math tex="J"/> factor <Math tex={"V_J"}/>, of dimension <Math tex="2J+1"/>. Permutations act irreducibly on <Math tex={"M_J"}/>. Each group acts on the other’s multiplicity space: the multiplicity of a spin irrep equals the dimension of the corresponding permutation irrep, and conversely. <Cite keys="childs-quantum-algorithms-notes"/></p>
 <h3>Three qubits: two copies of spin ½</h3>
 <p>The first pair can have spin zero or spin one. Adding a third spin-½ gives spin ½ from the first case, and either spin ½ or spin 3/2 from the second. Thus</p>
 <div class="formula-pair"><Math display tex={"(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes3}\\cong(V_{1/2}\\otimes\\mathbb C^2)\\oplus(V_{3/2}\\otimes\\mathbb C)"}/><Math display tex={"8=2\\times2+4\\times1."}/></div>
 <figure class="coupling-routes">
  <div class="routes-heading"><span>First spin</span><span>First pair</span><span>All three</span></div>
  <div class="coupling-route"><span>½</span><i>→</i><span>0</span><i>→</i><span>½</span></div>
  <div class="coupling-route"><span>½</span><i>→</i><span>1</span><i>→</i><span>½</span></div>
  <p>Two paths to J = ½ · two copies of a two-dimensional irrep</p>
  <div class="coupling-route symmetric-route"><span>½</span><i>→</i><span>1</span><i>→</i><span>3/2</span></div>
  <p>One path to J = 3/2 · one four-dimensional irrep</p>
  <figcaption>These paths label a coupling basis. A state may be a coherent superposition of paths; drawing a path does not mean that its intermediate spins have been measured.</figcaption>
 </figure>
 <p>A measurement of total spin alone cannot distinguish the two spin-½ copies. They transform identically under collective rotations but have different pairwise correlations. Exchanging the second and third qubits can mix the copies. Changing which pair we couple first changes the multiplicity basis.</p>
 <p>Young diagrams keep track of the same structure. For qubits, the row lengths are <Math tex={"(n/2+J,n/2-J)"}/>. At three qubits, shape <Math tex="(2,1)"/> has two standard tableaux and corresponds to spin ½; shape <Math tex="(3)"/> has one and corresponds to spin 3/2. The number of tableaux counts coupling paths. The difference between row lengths is <Math tex="2J"/>.</p>
 <details class="representation-detail"><summary>Why nested subsystem spins label the paths</summary><div class="details-body">
  <p>The squared total-spin operators for nested prefixes, <Math tex={"\\mathbf J_{12}^2,\\mathbf J_{123}^2,\\ldots,\\mathbf J_{1\\cdots n}^2"}/>, commute. Each successive label records whether adding spin ½ raised or lowered the total spin by ½. Those intermediate labels distinguish multiplicity basis vectors; the final label alone does not.</p>
  <p>Arbitrarily overlapping subsystems need not commute. For example, measuring the spin of 2–3 can change the previously certain spin of 1–2. In representation coordinates it is a different question about multiplicity space.</p>
  <p>Finite groups have finitely many irrep types. A compact group such as SU(2) has infinitely many, indexed by <Math tex={"J=0,1/2,1,\\ldots"}/>; only finitely many occur in this finite tensor power. For unitary representations, an invariant subspace has an invariant orthogonal complement.</p>
 </div></details>
 <p>The Schur transform coherently changes from individual-qubit coordinates to the labels <Math tex={"|J,m,\\alpha\\rangle"}/>: total spin, a spin basis index, and a multiplicity basis index. Efficient quantum circuits for this transform are known. Measuring only <Math tex="J"/> projects onto a whole sector; it is a different operation from performing the full coherent transform. <Cite keys="bacon-chuang-harrow-schur"/></p>
 <p>Every pair projector commutes with collective rotations, so it has the form</p>
 <Math display tex={String.raw`P_o^{ab}=\bigoplus_J I_{V_J}\otimes P_{o,J}^{ab}.`}/>
 <p>STP acts on the multiplicity factor within each total-spin sector. Starting from mixed input, the spin factor remains maximally mixed: no external spatial reference frame is required. The earlier Rudolph–Virmani construction supplied suitably chosen single-qubit states; their later theorem removes that input requirement. <Cite keys={['rudolph-virmani-relational','rudolph-virmani-stp','bartlett-rudolph-spekkens-reference-frames']}/></p>
 <details class="representation-detail"><summary>Algorithms for representation multiplicities</summary><div class="details-body">
  <p>The IBM article describes work on representation-theoretic multiplicities. Larocca and Havlíček use generalized phase estimation to obtain quantum algorithms in specified dimension-ratio regimes. Panova subsequently gave classical polynomial-time algorithms for many of the highlighted families, refuting some proposed superpolynomial speedups. The representation-theoretic questions remain useful; the classical comparison is part of the research, not settled by the existence of a quantum transform. <Cite keys={['larocca-havlicek-multiplicities','panova-multiplicities']}/></p>
  <p><a href="https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/group-theory">Ryan Mandelbaum and Vojtěch Havlíček’s account at IBM</a> is an accessible entry point. The primary papers state the algorithmic assumptions and subsequent qualifications.</p>
 </div></details>
</div></section>
